1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:09,050
Every night, when no one was watching, Hesper turned around.

2
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:14,750
She did not know why.

3
00:00:22,850 --> 00:00:27,800
Hesper was born in a field of ten thousand flowers.

4
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:34,800
From her first morning, she followed the Sun.

5
00:00:38,350 --> 00:00:42,550
East at dawn. South at noon. West at dusk.

6
00:00:46,350 --> 00:00:50,800
Every day, she turned to keep it in her eyes.

7
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:57,880
Every evening, it left her anyway.

8
00:01:04,350 --> 00:01:08,380
The old flowers did not follow anything.

9
00:01:12,350 --> 00:01:17,880
They stood heavy and still, their faces fixed on the east.

10
00:01:21,350 --> 00:01:24,800
Hesper thought they had given up.

11
00:01:28,350 --> 00:01:32,130
She promised herself she never would.

12
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:42,800
All summer, she chased the light.

13
00:01:46,350 --> 00:01:49,880
She grew tall. Her stem grew hard.

14
00:01:55,350 --> 00:01:58,880
Then came the morning she bloomed.

15
00:02:04,350 --> 00:02:08,050
And her body would not turn anymore.

16
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:23,050
The Sun crossed the sky without her.

17
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:31,630
It set behind her, where she could not see.

18
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:46,300
In the dark, facing east, Hesper thought: so this is giving up.

19
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:03,550
Then the east began to burn.

20
00:03:08,350 --> 00:03:14,380
The Sun rose, and the first light in the world fell on her face.

21
00:03:18,850 --> 00:03:22,050
The old ones had not given up.

22
00:03:25,850 --> 00:03:29,970
They had learned where the light returns.

23
00:03:36,350 --> 00:03:39,880
Hesper never chased the Sun again.

24
00:03:43,350 --> 00:03:46,550
Every dawn, it found her.

25
00:03:50,350 --> 00:03:53,630
Not all stillness is surrender.

26
00:04:00,350 --> 00:04:07,050
Young sunflowers really do track the sun, and turn back east each night.

27
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:12,880
Grown ones stop, and face east for good.

28
00:04:15,350 --> 00:04:23,220
In a 2016 study, east-facing heads warmed faster and drew five times more pollinators.

